  a"August 2016."  aIncludes bibliographical references.  aThis publication is a resource for practitioners seeking to build multimodal transportation networks. The publication highlights ways that planners and designers can apply the design flexibility found in current national design guidance to address common roadway design challenges and barriers. It focuses on reducing multimodal conflicts and achieving connected networks so that walking and bicycling are safe, comfortable, and attractive options for people of all ages and abilities.  aSponsored by U.S. Department of Transportation, Federal Highway AdministrationbDTFH61-11-D-00030g5008g5009  aDescription based on online resource, PDF version; title from cover (FHWA, viewed Mar. 12, 2018). 0aTraffic flowzUnited States. 0aTransportationxPlanning.0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85137042 7aTraffic flow.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01154142 7aTransportationxPlanning.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01155146 7aUnited States.2fast0(OCoLC)fst012041551 aUnited States.bFederal Highway Administration,0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n79032921esponsoring body.2 aCambridge Systems, Inc.0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no20090737722 aToole Design Group.0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n200504121808iPrint version:aPorter, Chris.tAchieving multimodal networksw(OCoLC)102935308640uhttps://purl.fdlp.gov/GPO/gpo898984 zAddress at time of PURL creationuhttps://www.fhwa.dot.gov/environment/bicycle_pedestrian/publications/multimodal_networks/fhwahep16055.pdf  3(online)uhttps://catalog.gpo.gov/fdlpdir/locate.jsp?ItemNumber=0982-V-01&SYS=001017101  aC0bGPO  aGPOO  abcb77 20180323  aBCS87  abc88 20180315b20180315  aPERM_INGEST_0428202202251cam a2200481 i 00101792920170831113437.0m     o  d f      cr cn|||||||||170516s2017    vauab   ob   f000 0 eng c  a(OCoLC)987330334  aGPObengerdaepncGPOdGPOdMERUCdAZPdGPO  apcc  a0982-G-11 (online)0 aTD 2.30:17