elopment, Federal Highway AdministrationbDTFH61-93-C-00051.20aLong-Term Pavement Performance Program (U.S.)0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no98068869 0aPavements, ConcretexJointsxMaintenance and repair. 0aPavements, ConcretexJointsxTesting.1 aRomine, A. Russell,0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no94015859eauthor.1 aRowshan, Shahed.0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no20040098042 aERES Consultants, Inc.0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n841084521 aUnited States.bFederal Highway Administration.bOffice of Infrastructure Research and Development,0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no99081415esponsoring body.2 aTurner-Fairbank Highway Research Center,0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n82270079eissuing body.08iPrint version:aSmith, K. L.tLTPP pavement maintenance materialsw(OCoLC)4269091908iMicrofiche version:tLTPP pavement maintenance materialsw(OCoLC)4355235140uhttps://purl.fdlp.gov/GPO/gpo924124 zAddress at time of PURL creationuhttps://www.fhwa.dot.gov/publications/research/infrastructure/pavements/ltpp/99143  3(online)uhttps://catalog.gpo.gov/fdlpdir/locate.jsp?ItemNumber=0982-G-11&SYS=001059752  aC0bGPO  aGPOO  abc76 20180604b20180604  aPERM_INGEST_0428202203222cam a2200505 i 00105976620180604142804.0m     o  d f      cr mn|||||||||150217s2015    dcua    oft  f000 0 eng c  a(OCoLC)903557192  aVVKbengerdaepncVVKdOCLCOdOCLCQdOCLCOdOCLCQdOCLCFdOCLCQdOCLCOdOCLCAdGPO  apcc  an-us---00aHE336.P43bS26 2015  a0982-G-94 (online)0 aTD 2.30/13:14-0991 aSandt, Laura,12aA resident's guide for creating safer communities for walking and biking /cLaura Sandt [and three others]. 1aWashington, D.C. :bU.S. Department of Transportation, Federal Highway Administration,c2015.  a1 online resource (100 unnumbered pages) :bcolor illustrations  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aFinal guide;b2014-2015.  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF cover (FHWA.DOT.gov website, viewed February 11, 2015).  a"Performing organization: Vanasse Hangen Brustlin, Inc. "--Report documentation page.  a"January 2015."  a"FHWA-SA-14-099"--Technical report documentation page.  aThis guide is intended to assist residents, parents, community association members, and others in getting involved in making communities safer for pedestrians and bicyclists. The guide includes facts, ideas, and resources to help residents learn about traffic problems that affect pedestrians and bicyclists and to find ways to help address these problems and promote safety among all road users. The guide includes information on identifying problems, taking action to address pedestrian and bicycle concerns, finding solutions to improve safety, and resources to get additional information.  aSponsored by Federal Highway Administration Office of SafetybDTFH61-10-D-00022 0aWalkingzUnited StatesxSafety measuresvHandbooks, manuals, etc. 0aCyclingzUnited StatesxSafety measuresvHandbooks, manuals, etc. 7aHandbooks and manuals.2lcgft0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20140261092 aVanasse Hangen Brustlin.0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no960035671 aUnited States.bFederal Highway Administration.bOffice of Safety,0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no2003014578esponsoring body.1 aUnited States.bFederal Highway Administration,0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n79032921eissuing body.08iPrint version:aSandt, Laura.tResident's guide for creating safer communities for walking and bikingw(DLC) 2014481178w(OCoLC)92067193040uhttps://purl.fdlp.gov/GPO/gpo924214 zAddress at time of PURL creationuhttps://safety.fhwa.dot.gov/ped_bike/ped_cmnity/ped_walkguide/residents_guide2014_final.pdf  3(online)uhttps://catalog.gpo.gov/fdlpdir/locate.jsp?ItemNumber=0982-G-94&SYS=001059766  aC0bGPO  aXZL4  abc76 20180604b20180604  aPERM_INGEST_0428202204366cam a2200529 i 00105977820191121113146.0m     o  d f      cr bn||||||ada160411e200012  vaua    obt  f000 0 eng c  a(OCoLC)946455586  aOCLCEbengerdaepncOCLCEdOCLCOdOCLCQdGPO  apcc  a0982-G-55 (online)0 aTD 2.30:99-2071 aHarwood, Douglas W.,10aPrediction of the expected safety performance of rural two-lane highways /cD. W. Harwood [and four others]. 1aMcLean, VA :bU.S. Department of Transportation, Federal Highway Administration, Research, Development, and Technology, Turner-Fairbank Highway Research Center,cDecember 2000.  a1 online resource (200 unnumbered pages) :billustrations  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTechnical report;bMay 1997-September 2000.  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF cover (FHWA.DOT.gov website, viewed June 04, 2018).  a"Performing organization: Midwest Research Institute"--Technical report documentation page.  a"Contracting Officer's Technical Representative (COTR): Michael S. Griffith"--Technical report documentation page.  a"December 2000."  a"Publication no. FHWA-RD-99-207."  aIncludes appendixes and tables.  aThis report presents an algorithm for predicting the safety performance of a rural two-lane highway. The accident prediction algorithm consists of base models and accident modification factors for both roadway segments and at-grade intersections on rural two-lane highways. The base models provide an estimate of the safety performance of a roadway or intersection for a set of assumed nominal or base conditions. The accident modification factors adjust the base model predictions to account for the effects on safety for roadway segments of lane width, shoulder width, shoulder type, horizontal curves, grades, driveway density, two-way left-turn lanes, passing lanes, roadside design and the effects on safety for at-grade intersections of skew angle, traffic control, exclusive left- and right-turn lanes, sight distance, and driveways. The accident prediction algorithm is intended for application by highway agencies to estimate the safety performance of an existing or proposed roadway. The algorithm can be used to compare the anticipated safety performance of two or more geometric alternatives for a proposed highway improvement. The accident prediction algorithm includes a calibration procedure that can be used to adapt the predicted results to the safety conditions encountered by any particular highway agency on rural two-lane highways. The algorithm also includes an Empirical Bayes procedure that can be applied to utilize the safety predictions provided by the algorithm together with actual site-specific accident history data.  aIncludes bibliographical references (pages 115-124).  aSponsored by Office of Safety Research and Development, Federal Highway AdministrationbDTFH61-96-C-00055 0aTraffic safetyxMathematical models. 0aTraffic accidentsxMathematical models. 0aRural roadsxSafety measures.1 aGriffith, Michael S.0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no20011014212 aMidwest Research Institute (Kansas City, Mo.)0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n790662761 aUnited States.bFederal Highway Administration.bOffice of Safety R&D,0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no2002057497esponsoring body.2 aTurner-Fairbank Highway Research Center,0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n82270079eissuing body.08iPrint version:tPrediction of the expected safety performance of rural two-lane highwaysw(OCoLC)4565042740uhttps://purl.fdlp.gov/GPO/gpo924224 zAddress at time of PURL creationuhttps://www.fhwa.dot.gov/publications/research/safety/99207  3(online)uhttps://catalog.gpo.gov/fdlpdir/locate.jsp?ItemNumber=0982-G-55&SYS=001059778  aC0bGPO  aXZL4  abc76 20180604b20180604  aPERM_INGEST_0428202203711nam a2200577 i 00105978620180604151108.0m     o  d f      cr |||||||||||180604e199910  vauab   obt  f000 0 eng c  a(OCoLC)1038532611  aGPObengerdaepncGPO  apcc  a0982-G-11 (online)0 aTD 2.30:99-1531 aWilson, Thomas P.,10aLTPP pavement maintenance materials :bPCC partial-depth spall repair experiment, final report /cT. P. Wilson, K. L. Smith and A. R. Romine.30aPCC partial-depth spall repair experiment, final report3 aLong Term Pavement Performance pavement maintenance materials 1aMcLean, VA :bU.S. Department of Transportation, Federal Highway Administration, Research, Development, and Technology, Turner-Fairbank Highway Research Center,cOctober 1999.  a1 online resource (118 unnumbered pages) :billustrations, maps  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aFinal report;bOctober 1993-June 1999.  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF cover (FHWA.DOT.gov website, viewed June 04, 2018).  a"Performing organization: ERES Consultants, a division of Applied Research Associates, Inc."--Technical report documentation page.  a"Contracting Officer's Technical Representative (COTR): Shahed Rowshan"--Technical report documentation page.  aSponsored by Office of Infrastructure Research and Development, Federal Highway AdministrationbDTFH61-93-C-00051  a"Publication no. FHWA-RD-99-153."  a"October 1999."  aIncludes tables.  aIncludes bibliographical references (page 75).  a"The Strategic Highway Research Program (SHRP) H-106 maintenance experiment and the Federal Highway Administration (FHWA) Long-Term Monitoring (LTM) of Pavement Maintenance Materials Test Sites project studied the repair of partialdepth spalls in concrete pavements. The purpose of partial-depth spall repair is to restore a pavement's structural integrity, improve its ride quality, and extend its serviceable life"--Technical report documentation page.20aLong-Term Pavement Performance Program (U.S.)0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no98068869 0aPavements, ConcretexMaintenance and repair. 0aPortland cementxEvaluation.1 aSmith, Kelly L.,0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no99075125eauthor.1 aRomine, A. Russell,0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no94015859eauthor.2 aERES Consultants, Inc.0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n841084521 aUnited States.bFederal Highway Administration.bOffice of Infrastructure Research and Development,0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no99081415esponsoring body.2 aTurner-Fairbank Highway Research Center,0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n82270079eissuing body.08iPrint version:aWilson, Thomas P.tLTPP pavement maintenance materialsw(OCoLC)4268459208iMicrofiche version:tLTPP pavement maintenance materials.w(OCoLC)4355454040uhttps://purl.fdlp.gov/GPO/gpo924254 zAddress at time of PURL creationuhttps://www.fhwa.dot.gov/publications/research/infrastructure/pavements/ltpp/99153  3(online)uhttps://catalog.gpo.gov/fdlpdir/locate.jsp?ItemNumber=0982-G-11&SYS=001059786  aC0bGPO  aXZL4  abc76 20180604b20180604  aPERM_INGEST_0428202203775nam a2200565Ii 00106035720180608101542.0m     o  d f      cr |||||||||||180608e199907  vaua    obt  f000 0 eng d  a(OCoLC)1039094975  aGPObengerdaepncGPO  a0982-G-11 (online)0 aTD 2.30:99-0861 aTayabji, S. D.q(Shiraz D.),10aPreliminary evaluation of LTPP continuously reinforced concrete (CRC) pavement test sections /cShiraz D. Tayabji, Olga Selezneva, and Y. Jane Jiang.3 aPreliminary evaluation of Long-Term Pavement Performance continuously reinforced concrete (CRC) pavement test sections 1aMcLean, VA :bU.S. Department of Transportation, Federal Highway Administration, Research, Development, and Technology, Turner-Fairbank Highway Research Center,cJuly 1999.  a1 online resource (64 unnumbered pages) :billustrations  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aFinal report;bFeb. 1995 to Oct. 1998.  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF cover (FHWA.DOT.gov website, viewed June 08, 2018).  a"July 1999."  a"Publication no. FHWA-RD-99-086."  a"Performing organization: ERES Consultants, Inc."--Technical report documentation page.  aIncludes tables.  a"Contracting Officer's Representative (COTR): Cheryl Allen Richter."  aSponsored by Office of Infrastructure Reserch & Development, Federal Highway AdministrationbDTFH61-95-C-00028  aAs part of the study reported here, analysis of data from the Long Term Pavement Performance (LTPP) GPS-5 test sections was conducted to identify factors that influence long-term crack spacing in continuously reinforced concrete (CRC) pavements and to determine the effect of crack spacing on pavement performance. Data from the 85 test sections from the GPS-5 experiment were analyzed.  aIncludes bibliographical references (page 55).20aLong-Term Pavement Performance Program (U.S.)0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no98068869 0aPavements, ConcretexCracking.0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh90005589 0aPavements, Reinforced concretexTesting.1 aSelezneva, Olʹga,0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n2001044539eauthor.1 aJiang, Jane,0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no2003070045eauthor.1 aRichter, Cheryl Allen.2 aERES Consultants, Inc.0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n841084521 aUnited States.bFederal Highway Administration.bOffice of Infrastructure Research and Development,0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no99081415esponsoring body.2 aTurner-Fairbank Highway Research Center,0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n82270079eissuing body.08iPrint version:aTayabji, S. D. (Shiraz D.).tPreliminary evaluation of LTPP continuously reinforced concrete (CRC) pavement test sectionsw(OCoLC)4188100208iMicrofiche version:aTayabji, S. D. (Shiraz D.).tPreliminary evaluation of LTPP continuously reinforced concrete (CRC) pavement test sectionsw(OCoLC)4348375440uhttps://purl.fdlp.gov/GPO/gpo924274 zAddress at time of PURL creationuhttps://www.fhwa.dot